WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.140
How do you become the highest grossing&nbsp;
director of all time? Easy — don't go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.300
to film school. These iconic directors didn't&nbsp;
need formal training to make movie masterpieces.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.400
If it wasn't for Peter Jackson, our vision of&nbsp;
Middle-earth would look a whole lot different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.140
today. Jackson's "Lord of the Rings"&nbsp;
adaptations won seventeen Academy Awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.300
across three movies thanks to their grand scale,&nbsp;
groundbreaking special effects, and epic scores.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.420
Jackson knew from a young age&nbsp;
that he wanted to be a filmmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.680
but he wasn't interested in attending school&nbsp;
outside New Zealand to make that happen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.389
nation's film industry wasn't substantial in&nbsp;
the 1980s, so he had to do things himself.

00:00:36.389 --> 00:00:40.920
"I never went to film school, so I don't even&nbsp;
know what film schools do, to tell you the truth."

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.220
After dropping out of high school at&nbsp;
the age of 16, Jackson worked hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.720
to earn enough money to purchase his&nbsp;
own 16mm camera. Jackson's first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.600
"Bad Taste," was shot on that very camera, and&nbsp;
premiered at the Cannes Film Festival in 1987.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.640
Jackson's career was launched with a film&nbsp;
that sits snugly in the splatter-horror genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.840
and he used his success to create more&nbsp;
of the same with 1993's "Dead Alive."

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.700
He gained international attention with&nbsp;
1994's "Heavenly Creatures," which opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.480
the door for his first Hollywood film, "The&nbsp;
Frighteners." Of course, it wasn't until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.080
"The Lord of the Rings" that the director&nbsp;
found himself thrust into the stratosphere.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.700
James Cameron is one of the world's most&nbsp;
successful directors — but unlike many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.180
of his peers, he doesn't have the&nbsp;
most consistent output. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.440
he spends years working on his projects,&nbsp;
often developing new technology to bring his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.700
imagined worlds to life. He's made several&nbsp;
of the highest grossing films ever made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.140
and there's a simple reason for that: he's a&nbsp;
brilliant storyteller who cares about the details.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.940
Cameron studied physics at a community college,&nbsp;
but transitioned to English before dropping out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.560
He then worked as a truck driver while pursuing&nbsp;
his interest in film technology. After seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.360
"Star Wars" in 1977, Cameron knew for sure&nbsp;
that he wanted to pursue a film career.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.040
Cameron's big break came when he sold the&nbsp;
rights to "The Terminator" for just $1. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.120
did this on one condition, though: he had&nbsp;
to be allowed to direct the movie himself.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.260
Years later, Rick Schultz asked Cameron&nbsp;
in an interview if film school was a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.040
place for aspiring creators to hone&nbsp;
their skills. The director replied —

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.800
"When I started out, I was working&nbsp;
as a truck driver. Didn't have much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.740
spare cash. Certainly couldn't&nbsp;
afford film school. But all you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.979
have to do is pick up a camera&nbsp;
and start shooting something."

00:02:28.138 --> 00:02:33.960
Lana and Lilly Wachowski, collectively known as&nbsp;
the Wachowskis, are two of the most unique and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.920
entertaining directors working in Hollywood&nbsp;
today. They made their mark on the industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.960
in 1999 with the release of the sci-fi classic&nbsp;
"The Matrix," which they co-wrote and directed.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.920
The Wachowskis followed a nontraditional&nbsp;
path to becoming Hollywood A-listers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.520
having started in the world of comic&nbsp;
books. Instead of going to film school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.180
they went to college for other subjects,&nbsp;
but they both dropped out before graduating.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.580
The pair worked in construction for a bit, and&nbsp;
in 1993, they wrote for Marvel Comics' imprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.680
Razorline's Barkerverse. They continued to work&nbsp;
in comics, initially conceiving "The Matrix" for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.620
that medium before transitioning&nbsp;
the story to the silver screen.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.280
Before that happened, though, they had&nbsp;
gone to Warner Bros. with the script to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.080
an action-thriller called "Assassins." The&nbsp;
film got a page-one rewrite and failed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.900
turn much of a profit, making&nbsp;
the Wachowskis realize that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.080
they wanted their visions brought to life&nbsp;
properly, they needed to do it themselves.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.940
They directed their first film, "Bound," for a few&nbsp;
million dollars, and it became a critical success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.900
After seeing what the pair could do behind the&nbsp;
camera, producer Joel Silver gave the green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.280
light to the movie the Wachowskis were dying to&nbsp;
make. Their third film for Warner Bros. was "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.300
Matrix," and the sisters have continued to make&nbsp;
movies in their own trademark style ever since.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.600
Ava DuVernay made her way to the film industry&nbsp;
much later than most of her peers. She was 32&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.740
when she picked up a camera for the first time,&nbsp;
and doing so was the result of a career change.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.300
She started working in journalism when she&nbsp;
helped cover the O.J. Simpson trial, but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.500
soon became disillusioned with the industry.&nbsp;
From there, she worked in public relations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.690
made a move to filmmaking in her early thirties.&nbsp;
In an interview with Refinery29, DuVernay said —

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:25.500
"Can a director also have my hair, and my skin,&nbsp;
and be a girl… Yes, everyone can be it all."

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.100
DuVernay's debut film, "I Will&nbsp;
Follow," was a small indie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.820
garnered great reviews and opened the&nbsp;
door for her to score some big-budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.000
projects. On the subject of her&nbsp;
first film, DuVernay elaborated —

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.200
Terry Gilliam was the only&nbsp;
American member of Monty Python,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.520
and he spent much of his time creating the&nbsp;
group's animated segments. He also performed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.780
with the group and directed "Monty Python and&nbsp;
the Holy Grail," which became a massive hit.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.040
When Gilliam started out, directing movies&nbsp;
wasn't on the horizon, as he started out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.900
a strip cartoonist and animator. He did this with&nbsp;
a BA in political science from Occidental College.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.640
Through his work with Help! magazine, Gilliam&nbsp;
met John Cleese, and after moving to England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.600
he met Eric Idle, Michael Palin, and&nbsp;
Terry Jones. Regarding film school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.320
Gilliam has opposed the concept,&nbsp;
having spoken about it numerous times.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.900
During an appearance on "Roundhouse" in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.340
Gilliam expressed the opinion that if someone&nbsp;
only studies the technical aspect of filmmaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.800
they'll be unprepared to get to work making&nbsp;
movies. In a Facebook post in 2011, Gilliam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.060
doubled down on his opinion of film schools,&nbsp;
saying it was for fools. The director wrote —

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.660
Quentin Tarantino is arguably the most successful&nbsp;
independent filmmaker of all time, and his work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.460
speaks for itself. His first directorial effort&nbsp;
was "Reservoir Dogs," which quickly established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.900
the young director as a force to be reckoned with.&nbsp;
He solidified his name in Hollywood history with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.040
his second film, "Pulp Fiction" — and from&nbsp;
there, he consistently made hit after hit.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.180
Tarantino didn't attend film school: in fact,&nbsp;
he dropped out of the ninth grade. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.020
he took acting classes at the James&nbsp;
Best Theater Company. He also worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.840
for five years at Video Archives&nbsp;
in Manhattan Beach, California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.834
where he further educated himself on&nbsp;
the artistry and history of cinema.

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:26.580
"I got hired because I was a film&nbsp;
expert. And then I had five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.520
to just do nothing but watch films&nbsp;
instead of get a job for a life."

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.880
Tarantino has discussed his lack of&nbsp;
formal training throughout his career:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.210
during a Cinema Master Class at&nbsp;
Cannes, he told audience members —

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.400
Tarantino expanded on this during his&nbsp;
time at the film festival, elaborating —

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.600
Those films made Tarantino into the&nbsp;
violence-loving auteur that we know him as today.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:59.700
Joel and Ethan Coen comprise the filmmaking duo&nbsp;
collectively known as the Coen brothers. Joel has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.840
been credited as director since their first&nbsp;
film, "Blood Simple," hit theaters in 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.980
while Ethan was uncredited in that&nbsp;
role until 2004's "Ladykillers."

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.220
Regardless, both brothers write,&nbsp;
produce, and direct — together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.580
they've created "The Big Lebowski,"&nbsp;
"Fargo," and "No Country for Old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.340
Men," but that's a fraction of&nbsp;
their outstanding body of work.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:22.020
Joel indeed went to film school, having&nbsp;
attended the film program at NYU. Ethan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.280
too, was no slouch in the education department,&nbsp;
but he instead earned an undergraduate degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.600
in philosophy from Princeton. If you've seen his&nbsp;
movies, that might not come as much of a surprise.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:35.100
Still, a philosophy degree isn't the same thing&nbsp;
as film school. At a press conference for "Hail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.700
Caesar!" in 2016, Ethan spoke&nbsp;
about his education, saying —

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:51.600
Julie Taymor has been ensconced in&nbsp;
the world of theater since 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.780
and it's a world she knows all too well. In 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.860
her adaptation of "The Lion King" became&nbsp;
the most-watched Broadway production of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.300
her generation — and according to Playbill, it's&nbsp;
the highest-grossing Broadway show of all time.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.320
Taymor didn't study film in college, as&nbsp;
it wasn't her primary interest — rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:12.060
dance and film have driven her throughout her&nbsp;
professional career. Her films are reminiscent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:16.260
of theatrical work, and it's easy to see how that&nbsp;
background drives her vision on the big screen.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.480
Taymor began working in film in the early&nbsp;
1990s. Her feature debut, the highly successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.460
adaptation of William Shakespeare's "Titus&nbsp;
Andronicus" called "Titus," gained worldwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:30.600
recognition. It did decently upon release&nbsp;
and has gone on to become a cult classic.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.380
Taymor followed up "Titus" in 2002&nbsp;
with "Frida," a biopic of the artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.860
Frida Kahlo. That movie earned&nbsp;
Taymor even more critical acclaim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.159
which she continued to enjoy with "Across the&nbsp;
Universe," "The Tempest," and "The Glorias."

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:46.500
Steven Spielberg is the highest-grossing&nbsp;
director of all time, and easily one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.820
the most influential directors of the last 50&nbsp;
years. Making movies was something Spielberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.140
wanted to do ever since he was a kid: he shot&nbsp;
a nine-minute film called "The Last Gunfight"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:59.640
for a Boy Scouts merit badge, and continued&nbsp;
shooting movies with his father's 8mm camera.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.040
Spielberg applied for the film programs at&nbsp;
UCLA and USC, but he was rejected because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.340
his grades. Spielberg wanted nothing&nbsp;
more than to be a filmmaker, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.660
and he wasn't going to let rejections from&nbsp;
film schools keep him from pursuing his dream.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.560
He attended classes at California&nbsp;
State University, Long Beach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.340
but dropped out when he scored a&nbsp;
deal to write and direct the short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:25.320
film "Amblin'." This early effort earned him a&nbsp;
seven-year contract with Universal Television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.500
making him the youngest director at&nbsp;
a major studio in Hollywood history.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:33.180
From there, he became the most successful&nbsp;
film director of all time — and he did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:37.620
without attending film school. That said,&nbsp;
Spielberg did return to college in 2002 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:42.240
finish what he started at California State&nbsp;
University, Long Beach. After 33 years away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.739
Spielberg finally took home a&nbsp;
BA in Film and Electronic Media.

00:09:46.739 --> 00:09:48.420
"But you're better at this&nbsp;
than you were in school."

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.304
"Oh damn, damn straight!"

00:09:50.304 --> 00:09:53.400
Stanley Kubrick is one of the&nbsp;
greatest filmmakers of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.820
and his work has influenced generations&nbsp;
of filmmakers. Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.840
Kubrick's interests weren't initially&nbsp;
as aligned with filmmaking as they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.160
with the technology and cameras used in the&nbsp;
process. His first career was in photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.540
though he didn't attend university to pursue&nbsp;
that dream. Instead, he sold prints to Look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.560
magazine shortly after graduating high school&nbsp;
and went on to become a staff photographer.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.320
Kubrick always loved cinema, and after some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.060
he left Look to make films on tiny budgets by&nbsp;
renting cameras and scraping together savings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.780
He studied the technical aspects of filmmaking&nbsp;
on his own, and after a great deal of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:29.280
he was able to push his way into the industry,&nbsp;
becoming an internationally recognized artist.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.800
Kubrick told Joseph Gelmis that working on every&nbsp;
aspect of his first two films helped teach him a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.670
great deal. When asked if he would attend film&nbsp;
school if he were starting over, Kubrick said —

